The check is `isProjectPath()` in `src/core/manifest.ts` (lines 42–46).

`readManifest()` (same file, lines 48–69) filters every manifest entry through `isProjectPath(cwd, f.path)` before returning it — so even if someone hand-edits `.agentos/manifest.json` to add an entry like `../secret.txt` or `C:\secret.txt`, that entry is silently dropped and never makes it into the `entries` map that `sync()` in `src/commands/sync.ts` later iterates over to delete stale generated files (the `rmSync(abs)` call around line 134).

`isProjectPath` itself does the actual guarding:
```ts
export function isProjectPath(cwd: string, p: string): boolean {
  if (typeof p !== "string" || !p || path.isAbsolute(p) || /^[a-zA-Z]:/.test(p)) return false;
  const rel = path.relative(cwd, path.resolve(cwd, p));
  return !!rel && !rel.startsWith("..") && !path.isAbsolute(rel);
}
```
It rejects absolute paths and Windows drive-letter paths outright, then resolves the candidate against `cwd` and checks that the resulting relative path doesn't start with `..` (catching `../secret.txt`, `foo/../../secret.txt`, etc.) and isn't itself absolute (catching drive changes via `path.resolve`).

So sync never even sees a malicious entry — it's filtered out at manifest-load time, one layer before the deletion loop runs.
